Q1. What is Abstraction? Why do we need it?

Interview Ready Answer

Abstraction is the process of hiding the implementation details and
showing only the essential functionality to the user.

It focuses on:

"What an object does"
rather than
"How it does it."

Why do we need it?

• Reduce complexity.
• Hide unnecessary implementation details.
• Improve maintainability.
• Improve flexibility.
• Allow multiple implementations.
• User only interacts with required features.

Java achieves Abstraction using:

• Abstract Class
• Interface

Real-Life Example

ATM Machine

The user can:

• Withdraw Money
• Deposit Money
• Check Balance

The user does not know:

• How PIN is verified.
• How the database is updated.
• How cash is dispensed.

Only the functionality is visible.

