Q3. What is the difference between Abstract Class
and Interface?

Interview Ready Answer

Abstract Class

• Declared using abstract keyword.
• Inherited using extends.
• Can have abstract methods.
• Can have concrete methods.
• Can have constructors.
• Can have instance variables.
• Supports single inheritance.

Example

abstract class Animal{

    void eat(){

        System.out.println("Eating");
    }

    abstract void sound();
}

------------------------------------------------------------

Interface

• Declared using interface keyword.
• Implemented using implements.
• Defines a contract.
• Supports multiple inheritance.
• Can have abstract methods.
• Since Java 8, can also have default and static methods.
• Since Java 9, can also have private methods.
• Cannot have constructors.
• Can only contain public static final constants as fields.

Example

interface Payment{

    void pay();
}

======================================================================

Q4. Can an Abstract Class have constructors?
Why?

Interview Ready Answer

Yes.

An Abstract Class can have constructors.

Although we cannot create an object of an Abstract Class,
its constructor executes whenever a subclass object is created.

Purpose

• Initialize common fields.
• Perform common initialization.
• Ensure every subclass starts with a valid parent state.

Example

abstract class Animal{

    String name;

    Animal(String name){

        this.name=name;
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal{

    Dog(){

        super("Dog");
    }
}

When

new Dog();

is executed,

Execution Order

Heap Memory Allocation

↓

Animal Constructor Executes

↓

Dog Constructor Executes

↓

Object Created

